  "lineage": "Argentine national folk-song repertoire codified in the 1940s-1960s by Atahualpa Yupanqui, Los Chalchaleros, Ariel Ramírez and Mercedes Sosa and centred on the Cosquín festival, comprising the slow 6/8 zamba with handkerchief-dance phrasing, the driving hemiola chacarera, the vidala and the chamamé, scored for nylon-string guitar rasgueado, bombo legüero played on head and rim, charango and quena, with declamatory rubato-heavy solo or duo singing in criollo Spanish.",
  "recipe": "classic South American mid-1940s folklore argentino, ballad Poetry, Latin am troubadour branch, iberian Latin protest song, andean carnival processional chilean nueva canción argentine canto nuevo / canto popular. thick, breath veiled tone, slow rate, interlocked note trading. cedar nylon fingerpicked classical nylon-string guitar, driving rim accented bombo leguero, naranjillo andean premium wood cedar andean andean classical charango, melodic quena, melodic straight piano accordion. large tracking room 1920-1965 mid tier odeon rca buenos aires. RCA 44-BX, German V72 tube pre, German tube console. 78rpm shellac.",
